1️⃣ Legacy API: rte_mempool_create()
======================================

    Old DPDK API (pre-17.x era)

    Only ring-based mempool was supported by default

    Memory allocation strategy was fixed:
        - alloc → internal hugepages / memzones
        - get / put → ring-based

    Limited flexibility:
        - Couldn’t easily plug in hardware memory
        - Couldn’t change allocation strategy
        - Couldn’t define custom object initialization easily

    Example:
        mp = rte_mempool_create("mypool", n, size, cache_size, priv_size,
                                init_cb, init_arg, socket_id)

    Always uses the default ring-based handler internally

    No way to swap the backend (alloc/free) without changing DPDK itself


2️⃣ Modern API: rte_mempool_create_empty()
=================================================

    Introduced for flexibility and extensibility

    Separates the creation of mempool structure from the allocation strategy

    Steps:
        1. Create empty mempool → mempool exists, no memory allocated
        2. Assign custom handler (ops) → can now define alloc/free/get/put behavior
        3. Memory allocated using your ops → supports any backend

    Advantages:
        - Custom memory backends (hardware, FPGA, NUMA-aware)
        - Custom allocation policies (alignment, zero-copy, DMA)
        - Multiple handlers in the same application
        - Legacy API still works → backward compatible
        - mbufs can use alternative handlers via RTE_MBUF_DEFAULT_MEMPOOL_OPS
        
        
        
        
3.Steps: 
===============

	1) Define our mempool struct : 
	
   
	  		 example: 
				   static struct rte_mempool_ops my_mempool_ops = {
					.name = "my_custom_ops",
					.alloc = my_alloc_function,
					.free = my_free_function,
					.get = my_get_function,
					.put = my_put_function,
					.get_bulk = my_get_bulk_function,
					.put_bulk = my_put_bulk_function,
					.enqueue_bulk = my_enqueue_bulk_function, // optional
					.dequeue_bulk = my_dequeue_bulk_function, // optional
					.opaque = NULL,
					};

    
    2) Register this ops: 
    		
    			RTE_MEMPOOL_REGISTER_OPS(my_mempool_ops);
    			
    			
    3) Then we call : 
    
			mp = rte_mempool_create_empty(...);			
					
		    rte_mempool_set_ops_byname(mp, "my_custom_ops", socket_id);

		Creates a mempool structure without allocating memory or defining allocation/free logic yet.
		The structure that keeps all the mempool metadata: number of objects, object size, cache size, socket, private data size, etc.
		But it does NOT allocate memory for the objects themselves.
		It separates mempool creation from memory management, allowing us to later assign a custom handler
		
		
		
4.  -d EAL option
=====================

1️⃣ What the -d EAL option does

    -d tells DPDK to load a shared library dynamically at runtime

    In this context, the shared library contains a mempool handler
    (a custom mempool ops implementation)

    Example:

        ./my_dpdk_app -c 0x3 -n 4 -d /path/to/my_mempool_handler.so

    Here:
        - DPDK dynamically loads the mempool handler from the shared object
        - The handler registers its mempool ops during load time

    Benefit:
        - Allows adding custom memory backends
        - No need to recompile the DPDK application
        - Handlers can be plugged in at runtime


2️⃣ Why order matters in multi-process applications

    DPDK often runs in multi-process mode:
        - Primary process
        - One or more secondary processes

    Each process must load mempool handlers in the SAME order

    Example:

        Primary process:
            ./dpdk_app -c 0x3 -n 4 -d handler1.so -d handler2.so

        Secondary process:
            ./dpdk_app -c 0x3 -n 4 -d handler1.so -d handler2.so

    If the order of -d arguments differs:

        - Shared memory structures (mempools, rings) may reference
          the wrong mempool ops
        - Handler indices will not match across processes
        - Results in:
            - Allocation failures
            - Crashes
            - Inconsistent or undefined behavior

    Rule:

        Always specify -d arguments in the EXACT SAME ORDER
        for all processes when using shared libraries



